# deploy/herald-notify-failure@.service
#
# Templated `OnFailure=` target, shared by every scheduled unit that wants this hook
# (herald-watch.service, herald-x-reconcile.service, and any later one). systemd's `OnFailure=` can
# only activate a unit, never a bare script (see `man systemd.unit`'s own `OnFailure=` example,
# which wires a notification script through exactly this kind of wrapper unit) — this file and
# herald-notify-failure.sh together are what the design doc's Files table calls the single "failure
# hook".
#
# Templated (`@.service` + `%i`), not a single fixed unit: this hook now serves two scheduled units,
# and a hardcoded target inside the script used to mean whichever one did *not* get the hardcoded
# name reported the wrong unit's name and tailed the wrong unit's journal on failure — see
# herald-notify-failure.sh's own header for the incident that forced this change. Each source unit
# sets `OnFailure=herald-notify-failure@%n.service`; `%n` expands to the failing unit's own full
# name (e.g. `herald-x-reconcile.service`) *before* systemd resolves the `OnFailure=` target, so that
# name becomes this template's `%i`, and `ExecStart=` below passes it straight through to the script
# as $1.
#
# NOT installed by any committed script — see herald-watch.service's header. Installing this is not
# additive: it replaces the old non-templated herald-notify-failure.service, which must be removed
# from ~/.config/systemd/user/ (not merely left alongside this one) as part of the same install
# step, or `herald-watch.service`'s own `OnFailure=herald-notify-failure@%n.service` (see its updated
# comment) will point at an instance systemd has never heard of.

[Unit]
Description=Herald scheduled-unit failure notice for %i

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/home/kyle/code/mantle-kr-herald
# A bounded backstop over the script's own internal timeouts — this must never be the reason a
# failure notice hangs, and it must never be the reason one is LOST either. Overrunning this does
# not merely truncate the alert: systemd SIGTERMs the hook mid-send, so the message never arrives
# AND herald-notify-failure@<unit>.service itself enters `failed`, which is the one thing a failure
# handler must not do.
#
# The script sizes its own internal timeouts as a sum against this number, and states the whole
# arithmetic under "The 30-second budget" in deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh:
#
#   2 × journalctl @6s + 1 × systemctl @2s + 2 × curl @6s = 26s, leaving 4s of margin.
#
# All five of those calls happen on one real path (no run log, Telegram unreachable). This is not
# "a backstop over the 10s curl timeout" — there is no single curl any more; the plain-text retry
# added a second one, and for one commit the sum was 32s against this 30s, measured, i.e. two
# seconds of guaranteed loss. Changing this number, or any of the script's three, means redoing
# that sum in both files.
TimeoutStartSec=30
# The deploy checkout, not the development tree. This unit runs a script by absolute path rather
# than through a WorkingDirectory, so it needs saying separately — and it is the path that matters
# most: a scheduled unit running unmerged code fails loudly, while a failure *hook* running
# unmerged code fails silently, because the only thing it would ever have said is that something
# else failed. Merging an edit to this script has already broken the live alert once for ~6
# minutes (PR #124). Updated by deploy/herald-deploy.sh; pinned by
# tests/deploy/workingDirectory.test.ts.
ExecStart=%h/.herald/app/deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh %i
